The scenario `env/env_0067_online_personal_loan_lender` is finished and passes every check. I ran the final checks myself:
- `validate_scenario` prints VALID with 0 warnings.
- The blueprint gates, BLUEPRINT OK and SHARE TOOLS OK, pass.
- All five difficulties pass all 8 gates, traceability included.
- All 7 products' docs pass the docs check (DOCS OK).
- No phone number, SSN last four, email, account number or customer name is shared between difficulties.

The scenario is customer care for "Larkspur Lending". There are 5 difficulties with 2 tasks each, plus 2 extra products (Payment Protection and credit cards) that no difficulty uses.

| Difficulty (product) | Policy audit | KB audit |
|---|---|---|
| personal_loan_servicing (loan) | clean, round 15 | pass, round 4 |
| hardship_assistance_plans (hardship) | clean, round 7 | pass, round 4 |
| practicepay_patient_financing (pos) | clean, round 13 | pass, round 4 |
| flexline_credit_line (line) | clean, round 5 | pass, round 4 |
| credit_builder_accounts (builder) | clean, round 12 | pass, round 2 |

The two extra products were rendered but never audited, by design. Their documents have only the render check behind them.

What made the run long:
- **Unrealistic rules in the first round.** Every difficulty failed its first audit, mostly because fee and relief rules were invented rather than what a real US lender does. Fixing that took many rework rounds.
- **Defects found after the audits passed.** In 4 of the 5 products, the document writer found mismatches between a rule's wording and its calculation, then stopped before writing anything. Each one went back for a fix and a re-audit.
- **Seeded data from other products.** A final cross-check, run once every product's docs were published, found that tasks contained records the other products' rules would never produce. Examples: loan payments worked out at the AutoPay-discounted rate instead of the base rate, and flat fees where the rules apply reductions. In the hardship and builder difficulties this changed scored answers, which were recomputed.

Decisions I made without asking:
- **Narrowing three rules.** In the loan courtesy waiver, the builder late fee and the pos relief priority, the reworker kept going in circles because the rule language can't compute what was wanted. I limited each rule to the cases it can compute and left everything else under the original base rule. Each change then passed audit.
- **Wording-placement notes left alone.** I didn't send back notes that were only about which document a sentence sits in. Each one is logged as a decision in the worklog.
- **Known base issues.** Three wording problems in the frozen base charters are logged as known issues rather than fixed. None changes any task's answer.

About 8 account cuts happened along the way; each interrupted call was checked on disk and re-sent or resumed. Everything is recorded in `env/env_0067_online_personal_loan_lender/worklog.md`, which ends with a DELIVERED line. I also saved this run's lessons to memory, chiefly to run the cross-product check as a standard step and to tell task writers up front to price other products' records by those products' rules.
